<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Luthuli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1898–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Luthuli was a South African anti-apartheid activist and traditional leader who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> from 1952. Elected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> chief at Groutville in 1935, he was deposed by the government in 1952 for refusing to choose between the chieftaincy and the ANC. Repeatedly banned, he led non-violent resistance inspired by his Christian faith and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, and received the 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1961. The official account was that a train struck him in 1967, but in October 2025 a reopened inquest ruled that apartheid police had beaten him to death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli.jpg" alt="Albert Luthuli" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Lutuli_Anefo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert John Luthuli was born in about 1898 at the Solusi Mission Station, a Seventh-day Adventist mission near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a> in Rhodesia, to John and Mtonya Luthuli. His father died when he was about six months old, and his mother, who had spent her childhood in the royal household of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>, raised him. In 1908 he moved to Groutville in Natal, where his family had lived, to attend school under the care of his uncle. After finishing his studies at Adams College in 1922 he stayed there as a teacher, one of its first African staff. His grandfather Ntaba had been elected chief in 1860 by the Christian community of the Umvoti Mission Station, and several relatives followed him in the office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Luthuli became secretary of the Natal Native Teachers' Association in 1928 and its president in 1933. In the same year he was asked to succeed his uncle Martin as chief of the Umvoti River Reserve. He took two years to decide, since the post paid less than one-fifth of his teacher's salary. He was elected at the end of 1935 and held the chieftainship from January 1936. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> in 1944. In 1946 he entered the Natives Representative Council, which adjourned that August in protest at the use of force against a mineworkers' strike.</p><p>He became president of the Natal branch of the ANC in 1951. In 1952 the government removed him as chief because he would not choose between the chieftainship and the ANC, and in December of that year he was elected President-General, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> as his deputy. He received four banning orders, the first on 30 May 1953. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a>, in which sixty-nine Africans were killed, Mandela and others argued for armed resistance. Luthuli at first opposed violence and later gradually accepted it for the movement, while remaining committed to nonviolence himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Luthuli received the 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1961. Under his fourth ban, issued by the Minister of Justice John Vorster and effective from 31 May 1964, he was kept from travelling even to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukuza">Stanger</a>. United States Senator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-f-kennedy">Robert F. Kennedy</a> flew by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> to Groutville in 1966 to visit him. On 21 July 1967 Luthuli walked from his house toward his store and field near Gledhow station and died on the way back. The official account at the time was that a goods train struck him, but another account holds that he was murdered. The Luthuli Museum opened on 21 August 2004, and the ANC's headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, Luthuli House, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert Luthuli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Albert Luthuli</b>, president of the ANC from 1952, led it in non-violent defiance rooted in Christian and Gandhian ideals, but after the Sharpeville massacre and the banning of the ANC in 1960, Mandela and others concluded that peaceful protest had been met only with force.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. Died in KwaDukuza, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-20.17,28.57&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Bulawayo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5320 2910 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert Luthuli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5770 3180h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5770 3180h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-20.17,28.57&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
